-- ============================================================
-- Pilates Physics: Content Seed — Video transcripts
--
-- Seeds 25 Instagram video transcripts as brain_entries
-- (type='transcript', is_active=false).
--
-- Default OFF: turning all 25 on at once balloons the Claude
-- system prompt to ~12K tokens. Toggle individual transcripts on
-- from the brain UI when you want them in the voice context.
--
-- The "title" stored is the same heading used in
-- video-transcripts.md so it's easy to find. The brain_entries
-- "content" is a markdown blob: a Topic line followed by the
-- full spoken transcript.
--
-- Idempotent via ON CONFLICT (type, title) DO UPDATE.
-- ============================================================

create temp table _transcript_seed (
  title text primary key,
  topic text not null,
  transcript text not null
) on commit drop;

insert into _transcript_seed (title, topic, transcript) values

-- ===== TRANSCRIPT 1 =====
('Pilates Springs Series — Part 1: Original Reaction Video',
  'Spring resistance charts are misleading without context; taller people move heavier weight in footwork',
  $tr$Y'all, this video is going around in the Pilates sphere and it annoys the crap out of me. Yes, you did get those numbers off of a balance body chart. That balance body chart has been around for years. You can find it in their catalog. You can find it on their website. But simplifying that chart into this video is completely misleading. And that chart actually shows the truth of the resistance which is as you extend the spring it gets heavier. So if you look at this chart here it is you will see that at 6 in it gives you a certain weight. At 12 in extension it's a different weight. At 18, it's a different weight. This is really important because if you have a tall person and a short person doing footwork, the tall person is going to move heavier weight even though they have the same springs as the short person because they're going to stretch the springs further. And that's a vital piece of information to understand if you're teaching Pilates and trying to progress someone in their strength training. I think understanding springs and how they work is crucially important to being a really good teacher. And so I'm going to make a couple other videos on busting some myths about springs and giving some realworld examples um and how I bring them into my teaching. But if you have specific questions about Pilates Springs, drop them in the comments below or send me a DM and I'll get back to you or make another video or whatever. Let's chat.$tr$),

-- ===== TRANSCRIPT 2 =====
('Pilates Springs Series — Part 2: Footwork Weight Differences by Position',
  'How carriage position changes spring load during footwork; real-world weight calculations',
  $tr$Welcome to part two of my series on Pilates Springs. Today, I'm giving you a real world example of the difference between the weight of the springs in footwork in two different positions. Now, here's me doing footwork. If I had three balanced body springs on, a red, a red, and a green, my pulses are between 12 and 14 in, which means the weight is 66 to 75 lb in that pulse range. But if I get tired and my pulses come closer, now I'm in the 6 to 8 in range. So I'm at 49 to 52 lb. So that difference in 6 in just dropped my weight down almost 20 lb. So why is that? Let's jump into it. So here's an example of me moving the carriage out on my Flexia reformer. And you can see that as I move the carriage out, the weight gets heavier. So let's look at the balance body spring weight chart. So on the vertical axis you will see the resistance and on the horizontal axis you see how far the spring stretched. So if we look at a red spring we can see that at 6 in extension it's about 16 lb. At 12 in, it's about 21 pounds. And at 18 inches, it's about 26 pounds. So, you want to know how I got to those numbers in the footwork example? Well, take a look at this chart where I've mapped out red spring and green spring at each of the intervals on the graph from balance body, and then we've just added them up. Y'all have been giving me great questions. I have so many more examples to share, so stay tuned. And don't be shy to ask for something else in the comments below.$tr$),

-- ===== TRANSCRIPT 3 =====
('Pilates Springs Series — Part 3: How Gear Bar Position Changes Maximum Load',
  'Gearing out reduces maximum spring extension and total load during footwork',
  $tr$Welcome to part three of my series on Pilates Springs. Today we're talking how adjusting the gear bar changes the maximum load from the springs during footwork. So first of all, I have this scale here. This is measuring spring extension. So spring extension is zero when the carriage is all the way home. So this is always going to be zero at the end of my carriage here. Remember that in first gear on balance body springs, red, red, green, I can achieve 18 in of extension, which equals 83 lb. Now, if I gear out, that means I'm changing the relationship between the carriage and the foot bar. The springs are still at zero at home. So, they're not extended because we've moved the gear bar. So, now I need to move my scale here so that I have accurate spring extension measurement. It's very high-tech. And now when I do footwork, let's see how far I'm able to push the carriage out. By gearing out the carriage or moving the spring bar 6 in, I reduced the total spring extension by 6 in. So that means the total load when I was geared in was 83 pounds if I had a red, red, and green balanced body spring. But when I geared out by 6 in, that total load reduced to 64 pounds. So, pretty big difference in maximum load. If you just love numbers and you want to know more common weights of balance body like footwork, spring setups, I've made a chart here that shows the weight at each level of spring extension. But I want to caution that this is not the end all be all like thing you should take to class with you. When I'm deciding what springs to use, I'm thinking about the intention of the movement. I'm thinking about the person's dimensions. I'm thinking about what their strength level levels are and how hard I want this to be. And so, this chart really should help you understand how springs differ with carriage positions and settings, like which ones you pick. But it's not the thing that you should the only thing that you should base your programming decisions on.$tr$),

-- ===== TRANSCRIPT 4 =====
('Pilates Springs Series — Part 4: Short Loops vs. Long Loops',
  'How strap length changes the range of spring extension and resistance during arm work',
  $tr$Have you ever wondered the real difference between using the short loops and using the long loops for a certain exercise? Well, let's try it out. So, I've got my spring indicator set up here where zero is where the springs are all the way closed. So, let's start by taking the straps and let's do a little serv tray. So, I'm in the long loops here. Now let's notice from the starting position to the end position, how far is the carriage moving? Now if I switch to the short loops, where's my starting position and where's the ending position? Because I'm moving in the middle of the reformer frame, it doesn't change the range of motion, but it does change the maximum and minimum stretch of the springs, which means the springs get lighter or heavier depending on the longer straps or the short straps. So, we can see how my servetry example plays out on the balance body graph for the red spring. So when I was in the short loops, I was moving from the 9 to 15 in spring extension, that area within the yellow box here. And then when I shifted to the long loops, I was moving in the 6 to 12 in range, which now the blocks has moved. So you can see that the maximum and minimum resistance for that spring has now changed based on the range I was moving in. Now, if you're curious how different spring weights from balance body would fare in this scenario, here's a handy chart for a single blue, single red, single green. Of course, this can apply to all sorts of different springs across all different brands, and the weights will be slightly different depending on how far you're stretching the spring.$tr$),

-- ===== TRANSCRIPT 5 =====
('Pilates Springs Series — Part 5: Does Body Weight Change Spring Resistance?',
  'Body weight does not change spring tension because of wheels; but body weight affects exercise experience through other factors like planks',
  $tr$Does body weight change the resistance of the springs? Let's do an experiment. For this experiment, we need a reformer with two heavy springs, a luggage scale, and a spring extension gauge to measure where 6 in of extension is. First, I'm sitting on the carriage and pulling 6 in out. And that luggage scale is right there between me and the rope. And it gets to about 18 pounds. Now, if I add my husband on the carriage, the luggage scale again reads about 18 pounds. The reason the resistance is the same whether you have one person or two people on the carriage is because the carriage has wheels. And the reason that body weight influences how hard it is to move something horizontal is because of friction. But when you have wheels with either bushings or bearings, friction becomes a tiny tiny tiny part of the equation and it's basically negligible in the Pilates setting. So, does body weight change spring tension? No. Yes, sometimes people of different body weights experience exercises differently. Totally normal. But it's not because the springs change their properties. Let's look at a plank for example. I'm going to take a few factors that could influence how they experience the plank out of it by saying they have the same height, they have the same spring settings, and they have the same reformer settings. So, gear position, foot bar position, and they're on the same reformer. But because they have different body weights, they might experience this exercise differently. It could be harder or easier. And one of the reasons for that is because in a plank you have to support your body weight. You have to lift it up. So you've got to get some force upward. And the other part is the body weight is actually pushing the carriage away from the foot bar. So you have to resist that motion. So when you're picking springs for a plank, there are a lot of factors to take into consideration. Um, and yes, the springs will be one of them, but not because springs inherently change properties or the carriage gets heavier by default because a person is heavier. It's because of all the other stuff that's happening. And a lot of times changing the springs will help get you to the intent you were aiming for with the exercise. But just understand, it's not because the carriage is all of a sudden harder to move.$tr$),

-- ===== TRANSCRIPT 6 =====
('Pretensioning Springs',
  $$What the second row of spring hooks does; pre-tensioning shifts the spring's working range slightly$$,
  $tr$Have you ever wondered what this second row of spring hooks is on your gear bar? Lots of reformers have them. So, let's talk about how that impacts your spring tension. When you hook a spring onto the second row of spring hooks, you're essentially pre-tensioning the spring. This is a really nice way to slightly change the spring tension without making a completely different spring selection. So, if you look at the graph for the balance body red spring and say we're moving over a 6 inch range, when you pre-tension the spring, you're basically moving that 6 in over on the graph by an inch and a half or two inches. That distance is the same distance as the first row hook to the second row hook. You do the same distance shift on the graph. So, in this example, you can see that the resistance of the spring really only changes a pound or two. And sometimes that's all you need. And sometimes that's like you can barely feel it. Personally, I really only use the second row of spring hooks for the extra light springs where we're talking about um a very light resistance. So that extra little bit of stretch does impact the experience a little bit more than say pre-tensioning a very heavy spring. But that's really my personal preference in the style of teaching that I do. Now some reformers have three rows, some have two rows, some only have one row. It totally varies. I think this is more of a like cherry on top in terms of reformer design. I hardly ever use it.$tr$),

-- ===== TRANSCRIPT 7 =====
('Standing vs. Seated Weight Comparison',
  'Why standing exercises move the carriage farther than seated (pulley mechanics); body weight and rope angle are negligible factors',
  $tr$All right, it is time to review the answer for this video that I posted yesterday. Pop quiz. Am I moving the same spring weight in the top and the bottom video? And the right answer is false. The spring resistance in the top and the bottom video are different. The basic reason for this is because when I'm standing, the carriage moves twice as far as when I'm sitting on the carriage. And one way you can think about this is the fact that when I'm standing, I'm stationary. But when I'm sitting on the carriage, for every inch I move my arm forward, I move backwards an inch because of the pulley. This is true for anytime you're comparing an exercise where you're standing, which is still, or you're on the carriage and using the rope, right? Because of the pulley. Again, it's all about spring extension. People in the comments gave me two other reasons besides the fact that the carriage travels more for why the weights would be different in those two videos. The first one was body weight, and I've already made a video about that. You can go check it out here. Um, and basically that's not a factor we need to consider in this example. And then the second one was angle angle of the rope. And yes, I do basically perform the movement either seated or like two feet higher standing, but it's not changing it for the reason that you think. Remember, I'm talking about the force the springs are exerting. And I don't want you to confuse that with the angle of the force which did change is working different muscles. What I really care about trig in this equation is does the angle change the overall spring extension. And yes it does. But the effect of that is super super minimal. Just like in my last video about body weight when I talked about friction. A lot of the things we think impact the forces we're feeling on the body are true, but they impact it so small it doesn't really matter. It shouldn't super come into your consideration when you're thinking about programming. I want you to think about the things that have a big impact on the experience of the exercise. Because if you can get those right, you're way more than 80% of the way there, which is great. But if you get the big things wrong, then it can throw off the entire experience.$tr$),

-- ===== TRANSCRIPT 8 =====
('Gear Position + Student Height',
  'How height differences and gear position affect spring load and joint angles in footwork; intention-based programming',
  $tr$Let's talk about height and spring weight during footwork. So, I've got Rose here up above, she's 5'11 and Andrea down on the bottom, she's 5'1. And they're both doing footwork on the same spring settings with the same foot bar height and in gear one. Let's talk hip and knee angles. So in this position, you can see that Rose's hip and knee are bent a lot more than Andrea's. If we look at both of them at full knee extension, you can see that Rose is able to press out a lot further, 20 and 12 in versus Andrea's 13 and a half. And because of that, you can see that Rose is actually moving 91 pounds at full extension versus Andrea's 69 pounds. So, if we look at Rose in three different gear positions, so up at the top we've got first gear, then we've got second gear, then down here we've got third gear. As you gear out, she's moving the springs less. So, we go from 91 lb in gear 1 all the way down to 72 lb when she's in gear three. Now, really, moving the gear bar out is a very bad way to adjust spring tension. I'd much rather you just hook a spring on or off. And the reason you might adjust the gear position is to change her starting joint angles. And you can see here again in gear 1, gear 2, gear three, her hip and knee angles change. So what is the right hip and knee angle to aim for? Well, that depends. It depends on your intention with the exercise. It depends on the comfort and the capability of the client. I want my clients to work in as big of a range of motion as possible under load. And so you have to be really clear about what the intention is and have a back and forth with the student about what they're experiencing. You can't skip that step because it's not about matching Andrea to Rose or Rose to Andrea. Neither one of them is the standard. There is an intention for the exercise and your job as an instructor is to understand the mechanics of the reformer so that you can help each individual achieve that intention. And while the mechanics of the reformer might not be as sexy as biomechanics of the human body or it might not be as easy as memorizing two reds and a blue for bridging forever for everybody. Um it's vitally important at the end of the day. We're here to help our clients get a good workout. Maybe they learn about their bodies and ultimately they're making progress. And that cannot happen if you don't understand how the reformer impacts the exercise for different$tr$),

-- ===== TRANSCRIPT 9 =====
('Rope Angle',
  $$Rope angle doesn't change spring tension (pulley rule), but changes horizontal/vertical force components; stability matters more$$,
  $tr$Does rope angle change the weight? Let's investigate. First, let's talk about pulleys. The rule of pulleys is that the rope tension on one side of the pulley is always the same as the rope tension on the other side of the pulley. And that's true no matter what angle the rope is at. In some of my last videos, many of you said that the rope angle would change how heavy the springs were. And while I just said that that's not true, one thing that is true is that depending on the angle of the rope, the vertical and horizontal components of that force change, which can affect how you experience the exercise. Here's what I mean. Let's take an example of servetry seated versus say high kneeling. So the angle of the rope is a little bigger when I'm taller. Now we can assume that the carriage travel is the same about 12 in. And on a single blue, that means the rope tension is about 14 lb. If I'm seated on my butt on the carriage, the rope angle is about 14 degrees above horizontal. Whereas, if I'm high kneeling, the rope angle is about 33° from horizontal. We've already established that the rope tension is the same for the high position versus the low position. However, the horizontal and vertical components are different. So, the amount that I have to push and the amount that I have to lift. Seated, the horizontal force of this rope tension is 13 12 pounds. whereas high kneeling it's 11.7 pounds. So that means it's a little bit easier to push when you're in the taller position. Looking at the vertical forces, seated is 3.4 lb and in the taller position it's 7.6 lb. So you can see here it's actually harder to lift from the taller position. So yes, the feel of the exercise does change depending on whether you're lower or taller. But I would argue that in the grand scheme of things, this has a very tiny impact compared to other programming choices that you make. In this particular example, the difference in stability between seated and high kneeling actually has a much higher impact on how my client experiences the exercise than the slight change in the horizontal and vertical components of the rope force. So, why am I doing the math here? It's not because I need you to achieve a certain number or that I'm expecting you to do your own calculations for every exercise. In fact, I'm not doing math when I teach Pilates either. I'm sharing numbers because I think it's important to demonstrate the real relative impact of certain programming decisions we as teachers make during a session. Your understanding of the forces in a Pilates environment is vital to the impact that your session is going to have. If you don't understand what's happening, you're just leaving the impact of your session up to luck.$tr$),

-- ===== TRANSCRIPT 10 =====
('Do Pilates Springs Wear Out?',
  $$Hooke's law, elastic vs. plastic deformation, spring lifecycle, and replacement recommendations$$,
  $tr$Do Pilates springs ever wear out? Let's dig in. Before we talk about where, we need to understand the rules that govern extension springs. And you might know that as Hook's law. And that is an equation F= kx. And basically that says the more you stretch the spring, the heavier the resistance. And it's linear. So for every inch, it goes up another certain number of pounds and so on and so forth. However, you can't do that for an infinite length. At a certain point, the spring will not rebound to its original length and properties. When you work within the safe length of extension of a spring, that's called elastic deformation. That means it returns to its original properties every cycle. Once you extend past this point, you get into plastic deformation, which is permanent overstretch of the spring and it won't return to its original length or properties. Now, most reformer springs, for example, are by definition working within the elastic range, the safe extension. However, that doesn't mean they can go forever and ever and ever. um there are only a certain number of cycles that a spring can safely and consistently stretch even in the elastic range of deformation. Now, if you look at garage door springs, these are a great example because they're everywhere. So, there's a lot of data, they'll say something like 7 to 10,000 cycles is the you the workable life of that spring. And Pilates equipment manufacturers have their own recommendations, too. usually one or two years for a normal studio. Um, and that's a great guideline and it's not because you're trying to they're trying to make more money. It is a safety recommendation. You don't want the spring to break and you also want to deliver a consistent experience. Now, it's not that when you get to a magic number of cycles, the spring just stops working. It gets there gradually and it's based on use. So, you know that when you're looking reformer spring. Certain springs get put on hooked up more than others. That's just like everybody has their preference or you're doing certain exercises and you pick the outer ones instead of the middle and outer one. And other things like um humidity and temperature or lotions and oils on your hands, touching the spring coils or letting the springs slam back on each other so the coils slam together. All of those things can accelerate wear as well. And so the answer is yes. Springs do wear out. It's gradual. You don't often notice it in real time. And the best practice is to regularly replace the springs in your studio.$tr$),

-- ===== TRANSCRIPT 11 =====
('Pulley Height',
  'How pulley anchor height affects rope tension and range of motion; why prioritizing ROM is usually the right call',
  $tr$Does pulley height matter on your reformer? I've received a few questions about this over the last few weeks. So, let's dive in. First, let's determine what you mean by matter. Do you care about the resultant force? Do you care about range of motion? Do you care that everybody is doing the exact same thing and weight? Or do you care about the angle of the rope? Your answer will determine how much you care about the height of the pulley. First, we need to acknowledge that when you anchor the pulley higher than the carriage, you're actually making the force, the rope tension, greater than the spring tension. And that's just basic trigonometry. The horizontal force in this triangle is the weight of the springs. And so then the rope tension, which goes up at an angle, is slightly greater. And we know based on the rules of pulleys that no matter what the angle is of the rope, the tension on either side of the pulley is the same. Now, let's put that into context. Here you can see me demonstrating the angle of the rope at three different positions. Home with my legs up straight in the air and then when my with my legs out at an angle um in feet and straps. When my legs are straight up in the air, the carriage travels about 14 in. So on a red and a blue spring for balanced body that is about 37 pounds. And if you have the pulleys anchored low like on a classical reformer the tension at my feet is 37 lb. But if you have the pulley anchored high the tension at my feet is slightly greater at 41 lb. The same comparison can be done with my legs low. The carriage travels 26 in. So therefore the spring tension is 57 lb. So, with a classical pulley setup down low, my feet are seeing 57 pounds of tension, whereas with the pulley higher, my feet are seeing 68 pounds of tension. Now, before you start lowering all your pulleys, ask yourself, why do you want one weight versus the other? Personally, I don't care because I know that my clients come in different limb lengths. So, no matter what I do, there's going to be variation in what each person feels, no matter what the equipment settings are. The basic rule of thumb you should consider is that the more you stretch the springs, the heavier the resistance is. And that almost always has the biggest impact on the weight in any exercise. Now, there are arguments that the line of pull changes the experience of the exercise, and I agree, it does change the experience. But I think that that change is okay. I'm okay with that change because I want to prioritize the range of motion. And when the pulleys are anchored higher, my clients have more room to move their limbs and the straps in a bigger range without them hitting their face or their chest or their shoulder rests. And I think that's just a much more pleasant experience.$tr$),

-- ===== TRANSCRIPT 12 =====
('Reformer Springs Across Contemporary Brands',
  $$Comparing spring profiles (light, medium, heavy) across Balanced Body, Stott, Align, and Peak; why conversion charts don't work$$,
  $tr$How do reformer springs compare across contemporary brands? Let's dig in. First, let's do a quick recap. Springs are not always one weight. You can't assign a single number to a spring. Springs get heavier the longer you stretch them. And the best way to represent this visually is with a graph. Okay, now that we've got that out of the way, let's jump into the actual brand comparisons. This is a graph of all the manufacturers lightest reformer spring. And you can see that across their travel, they're all within about 5 lbs of each other. Now, the 50% and 100% springs for these manufacturers start to vary between brands a little bit more, maybe about 10 lbs between them across the full spring extension. We start to notice that if we're switching between reformers, the most interesting graph is this one, the heavy springs. Now, there are two really interesting things that you can see from this graph. First, the initial tension of balance body start and a line are all very very similar. But as you stretch those springs, they get way more different. You can see those three lines getting wider as you travel on the graph. And then second, you can see that the peak spring is actually much heavier for initial tension, but by 36 in, it's one of the lighter springs of the four. So what does this mean practically? Well, the BB, the STO, and the Align Springs will all feel fairly similar for about 6 to 8 in of extension. But then once you get to 12 in of extension, they vary by almost 10 lbs. And that's practically really relevant because we are working in that 12 in of carriage extension range pretty often. Now, let's look at that peak spring. No matter how far the springs are stretched, the peak spring is always about 8 lbs heavier than the balanced body spring. So they have a K factor that's almost the same, but their initial tension is different. Now the peak heavy spring will feel heavier than a stop spring until about 20 in of extension. That's where those two lines cross where they feel about the same. And then for extension beyond 20 in, the peak spring will actually feel lighter. Now compare that to the align profile. The heavy peak spring will only feel heavier for the first 8 in of travel. At about 8 in, the peak and align heavy springs feel about the same. And then after that, the peak spring feels lighter. How do you apply this information in the real world? Now, if you teach on multiple brands at multiple studios, it's really worth knowing how the spring profiles are different. You can see that the light springs are nearly the same, practically interchangeable. The heavy springs are where the real difference lives. This analysis across brands shows that a simple spring conversion chart won't save you. Memorizing spring settings is not enough. What you actually need to know and understand is the intention of what you're teaching, how the specific equipment you're teaching on delivers load, and enough dialogue with your client to know when something isn't landing so that you can fix it.$tr$),

-- ===== TRANSCRIPT 13 =====
('Supportive vs. Resistive Spring Behavior',
  'A new framework for thinking about springs — do they help you or resist you in a given exercise?',
  $tr$There are a lot of bogus claims out there about springs and their properties and how you experience them in the Pilates world, and it's really frustrating, but I get it. The internet hates nuance, and we all want an easy way to describe what's happening as we're working out. Now, I want to propose a new way to talk about spring behavior. I'm not saying that I'm changing anything about springs. They still always get heavier as you stretch them. But sometimes when the springs get heavier, it helps you do the exercise. And sometimes when the springs get heavier, it makes it harder. So let's look through that lens to apply the labels supportive spring behavior or resistive spring behavior. So here are two examples. If you were to take bridging on the reformer, this is a great example of supportive spring behavior. This is because if you add more weight, it's easier to keep the carriage closed. So more weight is helping you. If you were to look at say chest expansion, the spring behavior there is resistive because adding more weight makes it harder to do the exercise to move your arms. Now, of course, there are caveats. There are some exercises like shortbox abs where changing the springs has no effect on how hard or easy the exercise is. And there are some exercises where you can apply supportive spring behavior to make the exercise intension one thing. Or you could treat them as resistive to make the exercise intension another thing. An example of that would be feet and straps. If you want to challenge the hip flexors and the abs a little bit more, the springs are supportive because adding more weight helps you keep your legs in the air. If you wanted to challenge your hip extensors more in feet and straps, then the spring behavior would be resistive because pulling the weight down is challenging the hip extensors and more weight makes it harder. Now, I love this definition because it sticks true to the laws of physics. Springs get heavier the more they're stretched, no matter what. Um, but it allows for nuance while still being clear and relatively simple with two labels about what's happening in this exercise. And the whole reason that I love this label is because it helps me as an instructor adjust springs during a session. If someone is having a hard time with an exercise, I know whether I need to add more weight or subtract weight based on whether the springs are helping them or making it harder for them. So that's just putting a couple of labels onto how springs interact with the body during a session.$tr$),

-- ===== TRANSCRIPT 14 =====
('Footbar vs Gearbar Adjustment for Footwork',
  'Lowering the foot bar vs. gearing out as options for accommodating tall clients in footwork; trigonometry explains why both achieve similar spring extension',
  $tr$In my video about adjusting the gear position for tall people during footwork, I got a lot of comments asking if adjusting the foot bar lower is also a good option. And today I'm going to show you why it absolutely is. First, let's look at the max spring weight. So, this is when someone's knees are fully extended. So, you can see that in the foot bar position middle in gear 1, the springs stretch 19 and 1/2 in. and that is 88 pounds. In gear two, on the same foot bar position in the middle, the springs extend 16 in. So that's 77 lb with full knee extension. Now, interestingly, if I bring the carriage back to gear 1 and lower the foot bar to the low position, at full knee extension, the springs stretch 16 in as well. So 77 lbs. It's the same as gear position two with the foot bar in the middle position. But if you look at it, I mean the numbers happen to be the same, but is it actually the same? Well, let's look at the starting joint angle setup. So, first let's compare the foot bar in the middle position, gear one, which is on top, and gear two. You can see that when you gear out, my hip and knee angles open a little bit bigger. Now, if you compare gear one with the foot bar in the middle and gear one with the foot bar in the low position, you can see that my hip and knee angles actually open a little bit more. So, even more than just gearing out. But then, how do the springs stretch the same distance? Well, the answer is trigonometry. when you gear out but you don't change the foot bar, your heels are at the same height between version one, gear one and version two, gear two. So when you are at full knee extension, the angle of your legs above horizontal is actually the same whether you're in gear one or gear two. But if we compare gear one with foot bar high and gear one with foot bar low, when you're at full knee extension, your legs are actually at a different angle. So that very slight difference in leg angle takes up the slack between gearing out which is just moving horizontally and lowering the foot bar which moves it a little bit horizontal and a little bit down. So what does this mean for your teaching? Well, it means that both options either lowering the foot bar or gearing out one are valid choices for accommodating a client in footwork. Personally, I'd pick the one that interrupts your group flow the least. And because I think adjusting rope length is super annoying, especially in the middle of a class, I'm going to pick lowering the foot bar as much as I can because I don't want to mess with the ropes and I also want to maintain as much carriage travel as possible throughout the class. And in the next video, I'll show you what I mean by preserving the carriage travel within the frame.$tr$),

-- ===== TRANSCRIPT 15 =====
($$Hooke's Law Basics$$,
  'F=Kx+B explained with a graph; spring weight depends on extension; foundational concept for understanding Pilates springs',
  $tr$Hi. Did you know that Pilates springs aren't just one weight? They're actually ruled by Hook's law. And today we're going to go over that physics principle. Hi, my name is Kaen. I'm a mechanical engineer and a Pilates instructor, and I teach other Pilates instructors about the physics of Pilates. So, what is Hook's law? Hook's law basically means that the more you stretch a spring, the heavier it gets. If you only take one thing away from this video, let it be that. Mathematically, Hook's law can be explained by an equation. F= Kx plus B. F is the spring resistance. K is the spring constant or the stiffness. X is how far the spring has been stretched. And B is the initial tension. Now, that's a lot of letters. It's kind of hard to visualize if you're not super into math. So let's look at it with a graph. Here's an imaginary blue spring from an imaginary Pilates company. Down here on the x axis or the horizontal axis, you have the spring extension, how far the spring is stretched. And on the y ais, you have the spring resistance or how heavy the spring is. And generally you can see that the more you stretch the spring, so you travel along the x- axis, the heavier the spring gets, which means it gets higher along the y- axis. So where do you get the K and the B on the graph? Well, the B is the initial tension. So that's where the blue line crosses that vertical axis at 0 in of extension. And then the K is the slope of the line. So for every inch of extension, how many pounds does the weight increase? If you want to know how heavy the spring is at a certain spring extension, you basically look at how high that line is on the graph. So here at 0 in of extension, that initial tension is about 12 lb. At 6 in of spring extension, it's about 20 pound. And at 12 in of extension, it's about 28 pound. If you want to know the exact weights for the springs that you're using, many of the manufacturers publish graphs or charts so that you can interpret how heavy the spring is at every extension it might be at. So, what does this mean for your teaching? Well, it means that no spring has just one weight. The weight of the spring is totally dependent on how far you stretch the spring. Some people may stretch the same spring further than their neighbor, which means they're actually moving a little bit heavier weight. And some people may stretch the spring less than their neighbor, which means they're moving less weight. Understanding this principle is vital for your teaching. I have a ton more videos that dive into specific scenarios in the Pilates world, so be sure and check them out if you want to learn more.$tr$),

-- ===== TRANSCRIPT 16 =====
('Physics of a Plank on a Reformer',
  'How body angle affects weight distribution and horizontal force in planks; supportive vs. resistive spring roles; progression strategies based on intent',
  $tr$Let's talk physics of a plank on a reformer. Planks on a reformer are a great example of an exercise where body weight and spring tension affect how difficult the exercise is, but maybe not for the reasons you might think. Let's take a look at three examples. First, a high plank with hands on the foot bar. In this position, the body angle compared to horizontal is pretty steep. So the lower body is taking a lot of the body weight and the horizontal force pushing the carriage out is quite big. Then there's a medium plank. Here the angle is a little less steep compared to the high plank, but the lower body is still taking a little bit more weight. So there's still a significant push horizontally of the carriage outward. It's less than the high plank, but it's more than the next variation. In a low plank with your hands on the floor and feet on the carriage, your body angle is nearly horizontal. So, it is the most balanced upper body to lower body weight distribution you're going to get. And the horizontal force pushing the carriage out is the least. So, what does this mean for your teaching? Well, in terms of setup, in a high plank, for example, someone with a bigger body weight is going to need a little bit more support than someone in a lower body weight because they have more force pushing the carriage out. That relative difference between body weights is going to decrease a little bit in the lower plank versions. In terms of progressing this exercise, I think about it in three ways, and they all depend on your intent. So let's look at the first two with a high plank. So say you want the intent of the exercise to be to keep the carriage closed, maybe more core focused. In that case, the springs play a supporting role. So you would remove spring support to make the challenge harder. On the flip side, if your intent is to load the shoulders a little bit more, the springs now play a resistive role. So you would increase the springs to increase the challenge because that makes pushing the carriage out harder. Or finally you could change the load of the shoulders. You could progress the intensity of the shoulder work by instead of changing springs going from a high plank to a medium plank and then to a low plank. I love this exercise example in terms of progression because it really hammers home the point of you need to know three things when you're considering teaching and progressing. First, you need to know the intention of your exercise. Second, you need to know how the piece of equipment that you're teaching on delivers load. And third, you need to know what your client is telling you, both verbally and non-verbally.$tr$),

-- ===== TRANSCRIPT 17 =====
('Gratz vs Balanced Body Reformer Springs',
  'Measured comparison of Gratz and Balanced Body springs; discussion of sample size, spring age, system-level differences, and why feel differs even when spring profiles are similar',
  $tr$Yesterday I asked you which one of these three black lines represented a Gratz reformer spring. And drum roll please. The answer is C. The line that is almost exactly the same as the balance body red spring. Now you may be rolling your eyes and going that's not what I experienced. And I want to say there's truth to what you're saying. But normally when we compare a GRZ and a balanced body machine we're comparing a system and springs are only one part of that system. Many of you asked some great questions in the comments yesterday and so here is some discussion on this case study. First, sample size. I measured two GRT springs, three balanced body springs and one green spring from Balance Body and that was just what I had. I spent zero dollars on this test. Second, spring age. The Grat Springs are about 10 years old, but they were brand new in a box. The Balance Body springs are also about 10 years old and they probably have maybe 250 hours of use. Do I think that's significant here? No. That's maybe less than 10% of their estimated lifespan from the manufacturer. Then repeatability. I'm trusting that the manufacturers have reasonable safeguards for their spring suppliers to make sure they're getting repeatable, consistent product. Fourth, intention. Here are the balance body springs compared to what Balance Body claims each of their springs are. The red springs are quite spot-on, and the green spring is a little off. I don't have any publicly available data for the GRT spring. I called them. I talked to someone, but it's been a week. I haven't heard anything, so I'm on my own about whether this is actually what they intended to produce. Fourth, changes over time. I have a lot of anecdotal evidence from people like you who are telling me that springs they bought 10 years ago new feel different than new springs today. And that's totally possible. Manufacturers are not obligated to tell us when they change something like a supplier or a spec. So, we're kind of out of luck here. I share all this not to invalidate what you're experiencing or you might be wondering like why the heck did I even do this experiment if there's so much discussion but this is how you ask questions and get answers and you do it over and over again until you have even more confidence and I want to add that in most cases when you're comparing a system springs are only one part of it you also have to consider wheels straps or ropes pulley and casters then things like design okay how far is the foot bar to the shoulder rests and that will influence how far people stretch the springs. Um, and then things like some classical springs have slack in the neck so that there's no tension for that first or last inch of travel. Um, and then also maintenance. If you're not taking care of your equipment, it's going to feel way different. So, why do this test? Well, first of all, I'm super curious. And I think adding numbers to this conversation is one step better than just saying, will they feel different? I want to add to this conversation about feel so that we can be more accurate with our language. And I think that can lead to being better able to interpret what we see and hear from our clients in front of us. It can help us work with more bodies and it can help us advance our industry as a$tr$),

-- ===== TRANSCRIPT 18 =====
($$Pilates Springs Don't Push AND Pull$$,
  $$Springs only pull (Hooke's Law); perceived "push" is a matter of body orientation relative to spring force direction; debunks the push-and-pull claim$$,
  $tr$Since I posted this carousel debunking common Pilates spring claims, I've gotten tons of questions about the claim "Springs push AND pull." To be clear, springs ONLY pull. But there are some situations where it may feel like they're pushing. Here's why. First, springs only pull. They do not push. The further you stretch them, the more they want to pull back to their original shape. They never "push" out to expand their coils. Remember Hooke's Law? This single-direction of resistance is similar to weights. Weights in the gym provide resistance because they act with gravity. They want to go to the lowest place they can be. They never "push" up against gravity. One direction of pull. Springs are not unique in this sense. However, both weights and springs can feel like they require you to push or pull based on your body position compared to the direction of the force. For example, chest expansion may feel like you need to pull against the springs, but footwork may feel like you have to push the springs. This isn't magic, it's just a simple problem of how you are oriented compared to the direction the springs are acting. Second, sometimes springs can provide support to a movement. Take a plank, for example. A medium spring is going to help keep the carriage closed more than a light spring. This may be confused with pushing, but again, it's just a simple body orientation, movement intent problem. And, if you instead put a weight on the reformer instead of a spring, it would ALSO provide support to the same movement. And finally, many people think that the "pulling" of a spring inward is better for eccentric muscle contraction than a weight. But this is a false distinction. Remember, concentric muscle contraction is the muscle contracting and shortening. Eccentric is the muscle contracting while lengthening. In cyclical exercises, there are both phases and they exist whether you have springs or weights attached. In fact, you could argue that springs are "worse" for eliciting both eccentric and concentric muscle contraction because they start off low weight, get higher weight as you stretch, then return to lower weight as you return. Whereas weights will provide the same resistance through the whole movement. But that is a discussion for another time.$tr$),

-- ===== TRANSCRIPT 19 =====
('About Me',
  $$Kaleen's background — mechanical engineer, Balanced Body, ACL injuries, Pilates changed her life, The Fit Reformer, Flexia, and why she teaches Pilates physics$$,
  $tr$Well, hello there. My name is Kaen and welcome to my internet home. In case you're wondering why I'm qualified to talk about Pilates physics and why I care, this video is for you. First of all, I am a mechanical engineer. I worked in the automotive industry and the orthopedic implant industry before I got my dream engineering job at Balance Body. Yes, that Balance Body, world's biggest Pilates equipment manufacturer. And as a way to learn more about what I was designing and building, I started taking Pilates classes there at work. And Pilates changed my life. I grew up a competitive soccer player and I tore my ACL when I was 15 and then again when I was 16. And even though I had surgery and did rehab, I still struggled with knee pain for a decade after dancing with my friends or hiking or playing wreck soccer. But when I started taking Pilates classes through work, uh, my knee pain basically disappeared and it was then that I decided I needed to learn how to teach Pilates, too. So, I started my teacher training in 2014. I left Balanced Body in 2015 and for the next 5 years, I ran my business, The Fit Reformer, where I did Pilates equipment maintenance in homes, gyms, and studios. While I was there, I created the world's first Pilates equipment technician training program, and I also developed a few courses to help Pilates studio owners learn how to care for their equipment. You might also recognize me from the BB Garage videos on the Balance Body website. Yes, I did test out of the Balance Body comprehensive Pilates teacher training program, and I also taught at a local studio. And my biggest influences for my teaching come from my mentor Amanda who is Cane School and Meline Black lineage. In 2020, I founded Flexia and launched the world's first smart Pilates machine. During this time, I raised millions from venture capitalists. I grew a team. We delivered thousands of reformers to homes across the country and ran a six-f figureure online Pilates studio. I sold Flexia in 2025. Now I'm here talking Pilates physics, teaching part-time, and building Remo, my next business, which is an AI note-taker for Pilates instructors. I believe that Pilates is magic, and I want to try and explain that magic. I also believe in science and evidence-based practice. And I believe that by better explaining Pilates, we can not only make our teaching better, but we can improve the quality of research surrounding Pilates. I'm not here to say whether one way of teaching is better than another or one piece of equipment is better than another. I want to argue for fact-based explanations of why things are the way they are in Pilates because if we start to base programming decisions on misinformation, it can derail the whole experience. Anyway, I'm glad you're here. Let's get back to the regular nerdy programming.$tr$),

-- ===== TRANSCRIPT 20 =====
('Intro to chair physics',
  'Why chair springs apply force at varying angles based on pedal height; spring stretch vs. pedal height is non-linear; vertical vs. horizontal force components; lower pedal = heavier load',
  $tr$Let's talk about the physics of the Pilates chair. We are about to level up our Pilates physics skills, y'all. You ready? Unlike a reformer where the springs apply force in the direction of the carriage movement, always on a chair, the springs apply force in different angles based on the pedal height. Because there are so many different kinds of chairs with different dimensions like petal length, petal height, height to the top of the chair, where the pivot point is, we are going to use the chair that I have, which is an older balance body combo chair. Look at some numbers and then try and create some principles that we can apply to other situations or other chairs. To be a great Pilates teacher, you don't need to know the exact numbers for every piece of equipment you're working on. You need to know concepts. So that's what we're looking at today. So, the first concept we need to recognize is that with a chair, we typically care most about how high the pedal is from the floor. But because it's an arm on a pivot point, as the pedal rises, it also gets closer to the front or the back of the chair. All that means is that if we change the height of the pedal by one inch, it doesn't necessarily mean the spring changes by one inch. With my particular chair, you can see that the relationship between spring stretch and pedal height from the floor up here is not linear. But if you compare that to reformer carriage travel versus spring stretch, you can see it's linear down here. Now, the other thing you need to remember is that for every force that's at an angle, it has a horizontal component and a vertical component. You may recognize this from some of my videos on rope angle on a reformer. Here's an example. On my chair, if I push the pedal halfway down, I'm stretching the two black springs about 6.4 in. That means the spring force is about 69.5 lb at 29° below horizontal. So the vertical component of that force is 33.7 lb and the horizontal component of that force is 60.8 lb. So why do we care? Well, we care because we want to know the vertical component of the force for this exercise. I weigh 180 lb. So downward force on the pedal is 180 lb. But the pedal force going upward is 33.7 pounds. So when the pedal is halfway down, at that point in time, I'm controlling 146.3 pounds over the whole pedal height from floor to top. The black line is the actual spring force, how far it's stretched, but it's always at a different angle. Then the purple line is actually the vertical component of the force at each of those pedal heights. Now, do you need to memorize this? No. Your basic takeaway is that the lower the pedal is to the floor, the heavier it is. And in my next videos, I'll look at how this concept applies to different body weights, different exercises, different body limb lengths, and also different spring Planes.$tr$),

-- ===== TRANSCRIPT 21 =====
('Body weight on a Pilates chair (Step Up Front)',
  $$How body weight changes the resultant force in step up front on a chair; same spring settings produce very different loads for a 250 lb vs. 140 lb client across the pedal range; why memorizing spring settings doesn't work$$,
  $tr$Student body weight can drastically change the experience of Pilates on a chair. Today I'm talking about how. Hi, my name is Kaen and I'm a mechanical engineer and I teach Pilates physics. Pilates chairs are awesome pieces of equipment and I love them in part because they take away a lot of the support that you get from a reformer. So, they're a great way to scale difficulty and look. However, we're often taught to memorize spring settings and that just doesn't work. It's like saying every pair of prescription glasses should have the same lenses. By sheer luck, it will work for some people, but for most people, it will give them a headache. Today, we're looking at how body weight can affect the Pilates chair experience. In Step Up front, the springs support the movement. So, as the pedal lowers toward the floor, the springs provide more resistance, helping you lift the pedal back up. Here's the setup we're looking at today. An older balanced body combo chair. Two black springs hooked onto the top hook in the back. and two humans having the same dimensions of limbs. However, one weighs 250 lbs and one weighs 140 lb. The first thing to remember is that body weight doesn't change the spring properties. The springs are going to provide the same load no matter where the pedal is in space and how heavy the person is. What we care about in this exercise is the resultant force. Basically, how much work or load is the person experiencing? We can break this down with simple math. the person's body weight is going down with gravity and then the vertical component of the spring force is pushing up against the person's body weight. So to find the resultant force, we take the body weight minus the vertical component of the spring force at the top of the pedal range just to open the springs a little bit. The springs provide 12 pounds of resistance in the vertical direction. So, the heavier person has to move 238 lb and the lighter person has to move 128 lb. When the pedal's in the middle of its range, the vertical component of the spring force is 47 pounds. So, the heavier person has to control 203 lb and the lighter person has to control 93 lb. And at the bottom of the pedal range, the vertical component of the spring force is 66 pounds. So that means the heavier person has to move 184 pounds and the lighter person has to move 74 pounds. So what does this mean? It means that if you give your students the same spring settings or step up front, they're not all getting the same challenge. Now, do you want all of your students to have the exact same challenge relatively? Yes. I want it to be hard for them, but that doesn't mean that they should all be on some ideal level of weight. We're not trying to get that. Everyone has different strength levels anyway. And you could have a really strong 250 pound person and a really weak 250 pound person and giving them different spring settings is the right move because they have different requirements to achieve a hard workout. So, if you've made it this far, here's my question for you. How would you make this exercise have a higher load for your students?$tr$),

-- ===== TRANSCRIPT 22 =====
('Load in a Bridge',
  'Three things change how hard a bridge is on a reformer — spring behavior (supportive), body weight (heavier clients face more outward force on the carriage), and shin angle (more horizontal shins demand more from the hamstrings)',
  $tr$Do you know how a reformer applies load to your students in a bridge? Let's talk about it. Hi, I'm Kaen. I'm a mechanical engineer and I teach Pilates physics. This is the movement we're talking about today, a bridge on a reformer. It has two primary components: hip extension and knee flexion. And reformer settings can change how difficult the exercise is. First, let's determine the spring behavior. Is it supportive, resistive, or not applicable? In a bridge, we've identified the hamstrings and the glutes as the primary movers. So, do the springs work in the same direction as the glutes? Well, our hip extension is going up and down and the springs are horizontally pulling. So, no. Do the springs work in the same direction as the hamstrings? Yes. Both the springs and the hamstrings work to pull the carriage closed. This means the springs behave in a supportive way for this exercise. If you add more springs, you're assisting your hamstrings to keep the carriage closed. If you take away springs, you're making your hamstrings work harder because they have less support. So, we know that springs can affect the difficulty of a bridge. But does body weight as well? And this will help me determine if I may need to offer heavier people a different spring setting than lighter people. And the answer is yes. Body weight doesn't just impact how hard it is to lift your hips. It's also creating a downward and outward force that's pushing the carriage away from the home stopper. So if you have more body weight, you have more force pressing outward on the carriage. So let's put some numbers on it to give some context. So in total, about half of someone's body weight is what we're working with to lift and lower. So someone who's 250 pounds has to deal with 125 pounds in the air and pushing the carriage down and out. Whereas someone who's 120 pounds has 60 pounds of body weight that they're working with in the air, pushing the carriage out. This means that with the same spring settings, the heavier person has to do more work to keep the carriage closed. And finally, what about joint angles? This is something that's primarily impacted not only by limb length, but of course, you can change it by adjusting the foot bar height or the gear bar setting. Interestingly, when the shin is more vertical, the hamstrings have to do less work to pull the body weight up. And when the shin is more horizontally angled, the hamstrings have to do more work. Now, there are other things that impact how hard a bridge is on the reformer, but they tend to be smaller or more edge cases, and I think we've covered the big ones here in this video. And if you're wondering exactly how this information is supposed to change how you teach, stay tuned for part two where I explain how physics has changed how I teach a bridge in a group us.$tr$),

-- ===== TRANSCRIPT 23 =====
('Physics and Teaching a Bridge',
  $$How Kaleen applies bridge physics in group classes — using supportive spring behavior to adjust on the fly, foot bar/gear bar baseline carried over from footwork, layered progressions, and her actual in-class spring-options script$$,
  $tr$Last time I talked about the physics of a bridge on a reformer, but what good is physics if it doesn't help you change how you teach for the better? Today I'm talking about how physics has informed how I teach a bridge on a reformer. There are three major components to this exercise. Spring behavior, body weight, and joint angles. Here's how each of those impact how I teach. First, we know that the spring behavior is supportive. That means more springs makes the exercise easier. So, I know that when I'm offering my students spring options or I'm making a change on the fly, that adding a spring is going to help a student who is struggling. Second, body weight has an impact on how hard the exercise is because the portion of your body that you have to lift in the air presses the carriage down and out. This means that when I offer spring options at the beginning of the exercise, I might mention this as something for my students to consider when they pick their springs. Third, joint angles. I have my students adjust their foot bar and gear bar when they're doing footwork. So, this is where I try and keep their reformer settings for the rest of the class. So, they're coming into the bridge with a baseline adjustment for their body. However, I know this doesn't always work. So, if adjusting the springs isn't enough to get them experiencing the intent of the exercise, I know that I can change the foot bar height or even the gear bar position to get them into a more comfortable and functional position. Here are three examples of how this might show up practically in my classes. If I have a very deconditioned client who has back pain, they may choose to do their bridge with their feet on the standing platform. My very tall and heavy client may need the heaviest springs with the foot bar lower one slot and the gear bar out. Or to progress my students, I might layer in variations and encourage them to stop at the one where they feel maxed out. First, we bridge. Then, we hold at the top. Then, we weight shift, making one leg do more work than the other. And then, we might even lift one leg off the foot bar. In my classes, I prioritize time spent moving. So, I don't start an exercise off by asking a bunch of questions, explaining all the theory, and giving each individual student their own settings. I take 10 seconds to explain the intent and the spring options, and then get them moving, and then I'll make adjustments on the fly. Here's my script. Change your springs for bridging. Your job is to keep the carriage closed as you lift your hips. You may feel this most in your hamstrings. The most supportive spring options are two reds and a green. And you might choose this if you cramp a lot. Baseline spring settings are two reds and a blue, and hard is two reds. Physics tells us that our students body shape, size, and strength impacts how they experience the exercise. And memorizing spring settings or reformer settings is a useful practice, but only with the acknowledgement that it's a great starting place and that as the clients get moving, you're going to have to adjust to meet them where they're$tr$),

-- ===== TRANSCRIPT 24 =====
('Bridge Press Out',
  $$Why pressing the carriage out in a bridge doesn't feel harder — torque on the knee increases as the shin levels out, but Hooke's Law makes the springs heavier with extension, and those effects roughly cancel; tested with a 20 lb kettlebell which exposed what happens without that scaling$$,
  $tr$In my last video on the physics of a bridge on a reformer, I explained that the more horizontal your shin angle is, the harder it is for your hamstrings. It's more torque on the knee. But if you're doing a bridge on a reformer and you press the carriage out, why doesn't it feel harder? The first concept we need to cover is torque on the knee. So when you're bridging with your feet on the foot bar, your shin tends not to be vertical. It tends to be at an angle. And because of that, we're putting more torque on the knee because there is a perpendicular distance between your heels on the bar and your knee joint. So the more your knee angle levels out, the higher the torque on your knee joint. If we compare these two versions of a bridge, you can see that the shin angle on one is steeper than the other. And because the equation for torque is force times distance, we know the force is the same. Distance changes. And because it the distance gets bigger, the more horizontal the shin is, the torque gets bigger. Okay, if that's true, then why doesn't the bridge feel way harder as you press the carriage out? Well, it's because of spring physics. Remember the spring behavior in a bridge is supportive? That means more springs make the exercise easier. And we know because of Hook's law that the more you stretch the springs, the heavier they get. So the further you move the carriage out, not only is the torque on your knee increasing, but you're also increasing the spring resistance, which gives you more support. So those two things kind of balance each other out. Cool, right? I have felt this phenomenon personally because I built a weight stack for my reformer so I could swap out my springs and use kettle bells for resistance. And let me tell you, when I put a 20 pound kettle bell on there, it feels exactly the same as springs when the carriage is closed. But when I press the carriage out, I about fall off because the load does not scale with the carriage movement, and my hamstrings are just not strong enough. Oh. Uh-oh. I'm stuck. Sometimes in Pilates, I'm annoyed that the beginning of an exercise is lighter than the end of an exercise. Why can't it just be hard throughout the whole range? And then there are other exercises like this one where I think it's pretty cool. The linear relationship between spring stretch and resistance makes it so that the springs support you throughout a range of motion in a way that a weight stack can't. My next videos will be comparing certain Pilates exercises on the reformer using weights and springs. So, if you have a request for a specific exercise to compare, let me$tr$),

-- ===== TRANSCRIPT 25 =====
('Force Vectors on the Cadillac and Tower: Spring Mount and Body Position',
  'Using force vectors (angle plus magnitude) to explain the two levers for adjusting load on the tower and Cadillac — spring mounting height and client distance from the spring hook — to match the load and spring angle across clients with different limb lengths',
  $tr$Today, I'm going to use force vectors to explain why we might change the mounting position of your springs or the client's body position on the Cadillac and tower. Hi, my name is Kaen. I'm a mechanical engineer and I teach Pilates physics. So, you've probably noticed that in your classes, client limb lengths vary a lot. And just because you have someone who's 6' tall and someone who's 5' one tall doesn't mean you can predict how long their arms are. I'm going to pick two hypothetical people here. I'm going to show you the height, not because it's always the same relationship, but because it gives some context to the picture. So, we're going to use a tall client with long arms and a short client with short arms for our comparisons here. When we're working on the tower or the Cadillac, we have two levers to adjust the loading of the client. We can change where the spring is mounted. And then you can also change the client distance from that spring hook. So, if we start with our tall client and our short client with the same spring mounting height, let's say they're going to prep for the 100 by pulling the roll down bar down. We're using two light arm springs with the clients positioned the same relative distance to the vertical bars, right? If they reach overhead, hold onto those bars. We can see that the springs stretch very different lengths. And as a result, the total spring weight is very different. at 8.3 lb per arm versus 6.3 lb per arm for the short person. And you can also really easily tell that the angle of the springs is very different between the two people. Now, there is not one ideal angle, but we always sort of use our own bodies as a reference point. So, if you're on the taller side and you want your shorter clients to feel what you feel in your body, we're going to use the taller body as the one we're trying to match for this exercise. So, the first thing we might try is just lowering the mounting point of the spring. that is going to change the angle of the spring. However, that means that the spring is not stretching quite as far. So, now we've made it almost um half as heavy as the tall person. So, we need to move the client away from the vertical poles. So, by doing that, we can now see that the angles are quite a bit more similar and we can achieve roughly the same stretch of the springs so that the weight is about the same. Now, this means we adjusted two things. where the spring was anchored and how far the person was from the vertical uprights. So now we've matched the loads um from the tall person to the short person. Now is this always what you want to do? No. There's no ideal load or angle. But as you know, you have an intention for what you're teaching. And so understanding the levers that you have at your disposal can help you help your client achieve what you are hoping to achieve. Now, if you have been with me for a while, you know that a force vector has two components, the angle and the magnitude. You can't have a force vector with only one. For that reason, this is a really useful mathematical tool to help explain the adjustments that are possible to try and change or adapt how you teach for different clients.$tr$);

